{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"cad7f1de84c38bc8","name":"card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/units/card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"25 POUNDER Mk I","text":"At the start of your turn, this and adjacent ground units get +1+1.","flavorText":"The Ordnance QF 25-pounder Mk I served as Britain’s primary field gun at the onset of WWII. It started as an emergency measure, created by mounting a new 25-pounder barrel onto the carriage of the obsolete 18-pounder field gun. Commonly known as the '18/25-pounder,' this hybrid was a compromise design. Although not perfect, it allowed artillery units to rearm quickly in the late 1930s. First issued in 1939, the Mk I was deployed with the British Expeditionary Force in France and saw action during the early desert campaigns in North Africa. It fired a 25-pound (11.3 kg) shell and offered both high-explosive and smoke rounds, along with a solid-shot anti-tank capability. However, unlike the later versions, the Mk I utilized a split-trail carriage rather than a platform, which limited its traverse and restricted it to a maximum range of roughly 11,500 yards (due to the inability to use maximum propellant charges). While it was soon replaced by the robust Mk II, which featured a dedicated carriage, muzzle brake, and 360-degree firing platform, the Mk I played a crucial role in bridging Britain’s artillery gap. Approximately 400 units were produced, providing essential firepower during the early war's most critical moments.","faction":"Britain","type":"artillery","rarity":"Rare","cost":4,"operationCost":2,"attack":2,"defense":3},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home_title","SourceString":"25 POUNDER Mk I","LocalizedString":"25 POUNDER Mk I"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::artillery","attack":2,"defense":3,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home_text","SourceString":"At the start of your turn, this and adjacent ground units get +1+1.","LocalizedString":"At the start of your turn, this and adjacent ground units get +1+1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home_history","SourceString":"The Ordnance QF 25-pounder Mk I served as Britain’s primary field gun at the onset of WWII. It started as an emergency measure, created by mounting a new 25-pounder barrel onto the carriage of the obsolete 18-pounder field gun. Commonly known as the '18/25-pounder,' this hybrid was a compromise design. Although not perfect, it allowed artillery units to rearm quickly in the late 1930s. First issued in 1939, the Mk I was deployed with the British Expeditionary Force in France and saw action during the early desert campaigns in North Africa. It fired a 25-pound (11.3 kg) shell and offered both high-explosive and smoke rounds, along with a solid-shot anti-tank capability. However, unlike the later versions, the Mk I utilized a split-trail carriage rather than a platform, which limited its traverse and restricted it to a maximum range of roughly 11,500 yards (due to the inability to use maximum propellant charges). While it was soon replaced by the robust Mk II, which featured a dedicated carriage, muzzle brake, and 360-degree firing platform, the Mk I played a crucial role in bridging Britain’s artillery gap. Approximately 400 units were produced, providing essential firepower during the early war's most critical moments.","LocalizedString":"The Ordnance QF 25-pounder Mk I served as Britain’s primary field gun at the onset of WWII. It started as an emergency measure, created by mounting a new 25-pounder barrel onto the carriage of the obsolete 18-pounder field gun. Commonly known as the '18/25-pounder,' this hybrid was a compromise design. Although not perfect, it allowed artillery units to rearm quickly in the late 1930s. First issued in 1939, the Mk I was deployed with the British Expeditionary Force in France and saw action during the early desert campaigns in North Africa. It fired a 25-pound (11.3 kg) shell and offered both high-explosive and smoke rounds, along with a solid-shot anti-tank capability. However, unlike the later versions, the Mk I utilized a split-trail carriage rather than a platform, which limited its traverse and restricted it to a maximum range of roughly 11,500 yards (due to the inability to use maximum propellant charges). While it was soon replaced by the robust Mk II, which featured a dedicated carriage, muzzle brake, and 360-degree firing platform, the Mk I played a crucial role in bridging Britain’s artillery gap. Approximately 400 units were produced, providing essential firepower during the early war's most critical moments."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/25_POUNDER_Mk_I.25_POUNDER_Mk_I","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":510.0,"Y":512.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_britain_25_pounder_home_photo","SourceString":"A 25-pounder with its wheels elevated on ammunition boxes for a very high angle of fire, Italy December 1944.","LocalizedString":"A 25-pounder with its wheels elevated on ammunition boxes for a very high angle of fire, Italy December 1944."},"blowUpExplosionNiagara":{"ObjectName":"NiagaraSystem'NS_ExplosionArmourM'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Niagara/NS_ExplosionArmourM.36"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeMedium_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeMedium.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackAr